window.CET4_BANKS.push({"id": "2020-09-2", "source": "2020年09月真题·第2套", "writing": {"prompt": "Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to write an essay on the use of PowerPoint（PPT) in class. You can start your essay with the sentence “The use of PowerPoint is becoming increasingly popular in class.” You should write at least 120 words but no more than 180 words.", "sample": "The use of PowerPoint is becoming increasingly popular in class. Walk into almost any lecture hall today, and\nyou will find teachers clicking through colorful slides instead of writing on the blackboard. This shift has\nclearly changed the way knowledge is delivered.\nOn the one hand, PowerPoint brings obvious benefits. Vivid pictures, charts and animations make abstract\npoints easier to grasp, and teachers save the time once spent on writing, so more content can be covered in one\nlesson. However, the tool also has its drawbacks. Some teachers simply read the slides word by word, turning a\nlively lecture into dull reading, while fast-changing pages leave students little time to think or ask questions.\nIn my view, PowerPoint itself is neither good nor bad; what matters is how we use it. Teachers should treat\nslides as a helpful aid rather than a script, and keep real interaction with students. Only in this way can\ntechnology truly serve learning."}, "translation": {"prompt": "茶拥有5000年的历史。传说，神农氏(Shen Nong)喝开水时，几片野树叶子落进壶里开水顿时散发出宜人的香味。他喝了几口，觉得很提神。茶就这样发现了。自此，茶在中国开始流行。茶园遍布全国，茶商变得富有。昂贵、雅致的茶具成了地位的象征。今天，茶不仅是一种健康的饮品，而且是中国文化的一个组成部分。越来越多的国际游客一边品茶，一边了解中国文化。", "sample": "Tea has a history of 5,000 years. It is said that, while Shen Nong was boiling water, several wild tea leaves fell into the pot, and the water immediately gave off a pleasant fragrance. He took a few sips and found it refreshing. Tea was thus discovered. Since then, tea began to gain popularity in China. Tea plantations spread across the country, and tea merchants became wealthy. Expensive and elegant tea sets became a symbol of status. Today, tea is not only a healthy drink but also an integral part of Chinese culture. An increasing number of international tourists learn about Chinese culture while sipping tea."}, "listeningUrl": null, "clozePassage": "It can be seen from the cheapest budget airlines to the world's largest carriers: Airlines across the globe  __26__  various shades of blue in their cabin seats, and it is no  __27__  . There does appear to be some psychology behind it. Blue is  __28__  with the positive qualities of trust, efficiency, quietness, coolness, reflection and calm.\nNigel Goode is a leading aviation designer who works at a company which has been delivering aircraft interiors for airlines for 30 years. \"Our job as designers is to reinforce the airline's brand and make it more  __29__  ,\" he says. \"But our primary concern is to deliver an interior that  __30__  comfort to create a pleasant environment.\"\n\"It's all about making the traveling experience less  __31__  and blue is said to induce a feeling of calm. While some of the budget airlines might use brighter, bolder shades, most others go with softened tones. The  __32__  aim is to create a home-like relaxing feel, so airlines tend to use soft colors that feel domestic,  __33__  and earthy for that reason.\"\nIt's also a trend that emerged decades ago and has  __34__  stuck. \"Blue became the color of choice because it's a conservative, agreeable, corporate shade that  __35__  being trustworthy and safe. That's why you see it used in all of the older airlines like British Airways,\" Nigel Goode added.", "matchIntro": "Why Are Asian Americans Missing From Our Textbooks?", "matchParagraphs": [{"label": "A", "text": "I still remember my fourth-grade social studies project. Our class was studying the Gold Rush, something all California fourth-graders learned. I was excited because I had asked to research Chinese immigrants during that era. Growing up in the San Francisco Bay Area, I had always known that \"San Francisco\" translated to \"Gold Mountain\" in Chinese. The name had stuck ever since Chinese immigrants arrived on the shores of Northern California in the 1850s, eager to try their luck in the gold mines. Now I'd have the chance to learn about them."}, {"label": "B", "text": "My excitement was short-lived. I remember heading to the library with my class and asking for help. I remember the librarian's hesitation. She finally led me past row after row of books, to a corner of the library where she pulled an oversized book off the shelf. She checked the index and turned over to a page about early Chinese immigrants in California. That was all there was in my entire school library in San Francisco, home of the nation's first Chinatown. That was it."}, {"label": "C", "text": "I finally had the opportunity to learn about Asian Americans like myself, and how we became part of the fabric of the United States when I took an introductory class on Asian-American history in college. The class was a revelation. I realized how much had been missing in my textbooks as I grew up. My identity had been shaped by years of never reading, seeing, hearing, or learning about people who had a similar background as me. Why, I wondered, weren't the stories, histories, and contributions of Asian Americans taught in K-12 schools, especially in the elementary schools? Why are they still not taught?"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Our students—Asian, Latino, African American, Native American, and, yes, white—stand to gain from a multicultural curriculum. Students of color are more engaged and earn better grades when they see themselves in their studies. Research has also found that white students benefit by being challenged and exposed to new perspectives."}, {"label": "E", "text": "For decades, activists have called for schools to offer anti-racism or multicultural curricula. Yet a traditional American K-12 curriculum continues to be taught from a Eurocentric point of view. Being multicultural often falls back on weaving children of color into photographs, or creating a few supporting characters that happen to be ethnic—an improvement, but superficial nonetheless. Elementary school classrooms celebrate cultural holidays—Lunar New Year! Red envelopes! Lion dancers!—but they're quick to gloss over(掩饰) the challenges and injustices that Asian Americans have faced. Most students don't, for example, learn about the laws that for years excluded Asians from immigrating to the U.S. They don't hear the narratives of how and why Southeast Asian refugees(难民) had to rebuild their lives here."}, {"label": "F", "text": "Research into what students learn in school has found just how much is missing in their studies. In an analysis, Christine Sleeter, a professor in the College of Professional Studies at California State University, Monterey Bay, reviewed California's history and social studies framework, the curriculum determined by state educators that influences what is taught in K-12 classrooms. Of the nearly 100 Americans recommended to be studied, 77% were white, 18% were African American, 4% were Native American, and 1% were Latino. None were Asian American."}, {"label": "G", "text": "Worse, when Asian Americans do make an appearance in lesson books, it is often laced with problems. \"There hasn't been much progress,\" says Nicholas Hartlep, an assistant professor at Metropolitan State University. His 2016 study of K-12 social studies textbooks and teacher manuals found that Asian Americans were poorly represented at best, and subjected to racist caricatures(拙劣的模仿) at worst. The wide diversity of Asian Americans was overlooked; there was very little mention of South Asians or Pacific Islanders, for example. And chances were, in the images, Asian Americans appeared in stereotypical(模式化的) roles, such as engineers."}, {"label": "H", "text": "Teachers with a multicultural background or training could perhaps overcome such curriculum challenges, but they're few and far between. In California, 65% of K-12 teachers are white, compared with a student population that is 75% students of color. Nationwide, the gap is even greater. It isn't a requirement that teachers share the same racial or ethnic background as their students, but the imbalance poses challenges, from the potential for unconscious bias to a lack of knowledge or comfort in discussing race and culture."}, {"label": "I", "text": "How race and ethnicity is taught is crucial, says Allyson Tintiangco-Cubales, an Asian-American studies professor at San Francisco State University. She added that it's not so much about the teacher's background, but about training. \"You can have a great curriculum but if you don't have teachers dedicated(专注于) to teaching it well,\" she says, \"it won't work as well as you want it to.\""}, {"label": "J", "text": "Some teachers are finding ways to expose students to Asian-American issues — if not during school hours, then outside of them. This summer, Wilson Wong will lead a class of rising fifth-graders at a day camp dedicated to Chinese culture and the Chinese-American community in Oakland, California. His students, for instance, will learn about how Chinese immigrants built the railroads in California, and even have a chance to \"experience\" it themselves: They will race each other to build a railroad model on the playground, with some students being forced to \"work\" longer and faster and at cheaper wages. Wong, a middle school teacher during the school year, hopes he's exposing the students to how Chinese Americans contributed to the U.S., something that he didn't get as a student growing up in the San Francisco Bay Area. \"I planted the seeds early,\" he says. \"That's what I'm hoping for.\""}, {"label": "K", "text": "And, despite setbacks, the tide may finally be turning. California legislators passed a bill last year that will bring ethnic studies to all its public high schools. Some school districts, including San Francisco and Los Angeles, already offer ethnic studies at their high schools. High schools in Portland, Chicago, and elsewhere have either implemented or will soon introduce ethnic studies classes. And, as more high schools begin teaching it, the door could crack open for middle schools, and, perhaps inevitably, elementary schools, to incorporate a truly more multicultural curriculum. Doing so will send an important message to the nation's youngest citizens: Whatever your race or ethnicity, you matter. Your history matters. Your story matters."}], "readingPassages": {"46": "When was cleaning a crime? When you're doing it to create art, obviously. A number of street artists around the world have started expressing themselves through a practice known as reverse graffiti(涂鸦). They find dirty surfaces and paint them with images or messages using cleaning brushes or pressure hoses(高压水管). Either way, it's the same principle: the image is made by cleaning away the dirt. Each artist has their own individual style but all artists share a common aim: to draw attention to the pollution in our cities. The UK's Paul Curtis, better known as Moose, operates around Leeds and London and has been commissioned by a number of companies to make reverse graffiti advertisements.\nBrazilian artist, Alexandre Orion, turned one of Sao Paulo's transport tunnels into an amazing wall painting in 2006 by getting rid of the dirt. Made up of a series of white skulls(颅骨), the painting reminds drivers of the effect their pollution is having on the planet. \"Every motorist sits in the comfort of their car, but they don't give any consideration to the price their comfort has for the environment and consequently for themselves,\" says Orion.\nThe anti-pollution message of the reverse graffiti artists confuses city authorities since the main argument against graffiti is that it spoils the appearance of both types of property: public and private. This was what Leeds City Council said about Moose's work: \"Leeds residents want to live in clean and attractive neighbourhoods. We view this kind of advertising as environmental damage and will take strong action against it.\" Moose was ordered to \"clean up his act.\" How was he supposed to do this: by making all property he had cleaned dirty again?\nAs for the Brazilian artist's work, the authorities were annoyed but could find nothing to charge him with. They had no other option but to clean the tunnel—but only the parts Alexandre had already cleaned. The artist merely continued his campaign on the other side. The city officials then decided to take drastic action. They not only cleaned the whole tunnel but every tunnel in Sao Paulo.", "47": "When was cleaning a crime? When you're doing it to create art, obviously. A number of street artists around the world have started expressing themselves through a practice known as reverse graffiti(涂鸦). They find dirty surfaces and paint them with images or messages using cleaning brushes or pressure hoses(高压水管). Either way, it's the same principle: the image is made by cleaning away the dirt. Each artist has their own individual style but all artists share a common aim: to draw attention to the pollution in our cities. The UK's Paul Curtis, better known as Moose, operates around Leeds and London and has been commissioned by a number of companies to make reverse graffiti advertisements.\nBrazilian artist, Alexandre Orion, turned one of Sao Paulo's transport tunnels into an amazing wall painting in 2006 by getting rid of the dirt. Made up of a series of white skulls(颅骨), the painting reminds drivers of the effect their pollution is having on the planet. \"Every motorist sits in the comfort of their car, but they don't give any consideration to the price their comfort has for the environment and consequently for themselves,\" says Orion.\nThe anti-pollution message of the reverse graffiti artists confuses city authorities since the main argument against graffiti is that it spoils the appearance of both types of property: public and private. This was what Leeds City Council said about Moose's work: \"Leeds residents want to live in clean and attractive neighbourhoods. We view this kind of advertising as environmental damage and will take strong action against it.\" Moose was ordered to \"clean up his act.\" How was he supposed to do this: by making all property he had cleaned dirty again?\nAs for the Brazilian artist's work, the authorities were annoyed but could find nothing to charge him with. They had no other option but to clean the tunnel—but only the parts Alexandre had already cleaned. The artist merely continued his campaign on the other side. The city officials then decided to take drastic action. They not only cleaned the whole tunnel but every tunnel in Sao Paulo.", "48": "When was cleaning a crime? When you're doing it to create art, obviously. A number of street artists around the world have started expressing themselves through a practice known as reverse graffiti(涂鸦). They find dirty surfaces and paint them with images or messages using cleaning brushes or pressure hoses(高压水管). Either way, it's the same principle: the image is made by cleaning away the dirt. Each artist has their own individual style but all artists share a common aim: to draw attention to the pollution in our cities. The UK's Paul Curtis, better known as Moose, operates around Leeds and London and has been commissioned by a number of companies to make reverse graffiti advertisements.\nBrazilian artist, Alexandre Orion, turned one of Sao Paulo's transport tunnels into an amazing wall painting in 2006 by getting rid of the dirt. Made up of a series of white skulls(颅骨), the painting reminds drivers of the effect their pollution is having on the planet. \"Every motorist sits in the comfort of their car, but they don't give any consideration to the price their comfort has for the environment and consequently for themselves,\" says Orion.\nThe anti-pollution message of the reverse graffiti artists confuses city authorities since the main argument against graffiti is that it spoils the appearance of both types of property: public and private. This was what Leeds City Council said about Moose's work: \"Leeds residents want to live in clean and attractive neighbourhoods. We view this kind of advertising as environmental damage and will take strong action against it.\" Moose was ordered to \"clean up his act.\" How was he supposed to do this: by making all property he had cleaned dirty again?\nAs for the Brazilian artist's work, the authorities were annoyed but could find nothing to charge him with. They had no other option but to clean the tunnel—but only the parts Alexandre had already cleaned. The artist merely continued his campaign on the other side. The city officials then decided to take drastic action. They not only cleaned the whole tunnel but every tunnel in Sao Paulo.", "49": "When was cleaning a crime? When you're doing it to create art, obviously. A number of street artists around the world have started expressing themselves through a practice known as reverse graffiti(涂鸦). They find dirty surfaces and paint them with images or messages using cleaning brushes or pressure hoses(高压水管). Either way, it's the same principle: the image is made by cleaning away the dirt. Each artist has their own individual style but all artists share a common aim: to draw attention to the pollution in our cities. The UK's Paul Curtis, better known as Moose, operates around Leeds and London and has been commissioned by a number of companies to make reverse graffiti advertisements.\nBrazilian artist, Alexandre Orion, turned one of Sao Paulo's transport tunnels into an amazing wall painting in 2006 by getting rid of the dirt. Made up of a series of white skulls(颅骨), the painting reminds drivers of the effect their pollution is having on the planet. \"Every motorist sits in the comfort of their car, but they don't give any consideration to the price their comfort has for the environment and consequently for themselves,\" says Orion.\nThe anti-pollution message of the reverse graffiti artists confuses city authorities since the main argument against graffiti is that it spoils the appearance of both types of property: public and private. This was what Leeds City Council said about Moose's work: \"Leeds residents want to live in clean and attractive neighbourhoods. We view this kind of advertising as environmental damage and will take strong action against it.\" Moose was ordered to \"clean up his act.\" How was he supposed to do this: by making all property he had cleaned dirty again?\nAs for the Brazilian artist's work, the authorities were annoyed but could find nothing to charge him with. They had no other option but to clean the tunnel—but only the parts Alexandre had already cleaned. The artist merely continued his campaign on the other side. The city officials then decided to take drastic action. They not only cleaned the whole tunnel but every tunnel in Sao Paulo.", "50": "When was cleaning a crime? When you're doing it to create art, obviously. A number of street artists around the world have started expressing themselves through a practice known as reverse graffiti(涂鸦). They find dirty surfaces and paint them with images or messages using cleaning brushes or pressure hoses(高压水管). Either way, it's the same principle: the image is made by cleaning away the dirt. Each artist has their own individual style but all artists share a common aim: to draw attention to the pollution in our cities. The UK's Paul Curtis, better known as Moose, operates around Leeds and London and has been commissioned by a number of companies to make reverse graffiti advertisements.\nBrazilian artist, Alexandre Orion, turned one of Sao Paulo's transport tunnels into an amazing wall painting in 2006 by getting rid of the dirt. Made up of a series of white skulls(颅骨), the painting reminds drivers of the effect their pollution is having on the planet. \"Every motorist sits in the comfort of their car, but they don't give any consideration to the price their comfort has for the environment and consequently for themselves,\" says Orion.\nThe anti-pollution message of the reverse graffiti artists confuses city authorities since the main argument against graffiti is that it spoils the appearance of both types of property: public and private. This was what Leeds City Council said about Moose's work: \"Leeds residents want to live in clean and attractive neighbourhoods. We view this kind of advertising as environmental damage and will take strong action against it.\" Moose was ordered to \"clean up his act.\" How was he supposed to do this: by making all property he had cleaned dirty again?\nAs for the Brazilian artist's work, the authorities were annoyed but could find nothing to charge him with. They had no other option but to clean the tunnel—but only the parts Alexandre had already cleaned. The artist merely continued his campaign on the other side. The city officials then decided to take drastic action. They not only cleaned the whole tunnel but every tunnel in Sao Paulo.", "51": "The practice of paying children an allowance became popular in America about 100 years ago. Nowadays, American kids on average receive about $800 per year in allowance. But the vast majority of American parents who pay allowance tie it to the completion of housework. Although many parents believe that paying an allowance for completing chores benefits their children, a range of experts expressed concern that tying allowance very closely to chores may not be ideal. In fact, the way chores work in many households worldwide points to another way.\nSuniya Luthar, a psychologist, is against paying kids for chores. Luthar is not opposed to giving allowances, but she thinks it's important to establish that chores are done not because they will lead to payment, but because they keep the household running. Luthar's suggested approach to allowance is compatible with that of writer Ron Lieber, who advises that allowances be used as a means of showing children how to save, give, and spend on things they care about. Kids should do chores, he writes, \"for the same reason adults do, because the chores need to be done, and not with the expectation of compensation.\"\nThis argument has its critics, but considering the way chores are undertaken around the world may change people's thinking. Professor David Lancy of Utah State University has studied how families around the world handle chores. At about 18 months of age, Lancy says, most children become eager to help their parents, and in many cultures, they begin helping with housework at that age. They begin with very simple tasks, but their responsibilities gradually increase. And they do these tasks without payment. Lancy contrasts this with what happens in America. \"We deny our children's bids to help until they are 6 or 7 years old,\" Lancy says, \"when many have lost the desire to help and then try to motivate them with payment. The solution to this problem is not to try to use money as an incentive to do housework, but to get children involved in housework much earlier, when they actually want to do it.\"", "52": "The practice of paying children an allowance became popular in America about 100 years ago. Nowadays, American kids on average receive about $800 per year in allowance. But the vast majority of American parents who pay allowance tie it to the completion of housework. Although many parents believe that paying an allowance for completing chores benefits their children, a range of experts expressed concern that tying allowance very closely to chores may not be ideal. In fact, the way chores work in many households worldwide points to another way.\nSuniya Luthar, a psychologist, is against paying kids for chores. Luthar is not opposed to giving allowances, but she thinks it's important to establish that chores are done not because they will lead to payment, but because they keep the household running. Luthar's suggested approach to allowance is compatible with that of writer Ron Lieber, who advises that allowances be used as a means of showing children how to save, give, and spend on things they care about. Kids should do chores, he writes, \"for the same reason adults do, because the chores need to be done, and not with the expectation of compensation.\"\nThis argument has its critics, but considering the way chores are undertaken around the world may change people's thinking. Professor David Lancy of Utah State University has studied how families around the world handle chores. At about 18 months of age, Lancy says, most children become eager to help their parents, and in many cultures, they begin helping with housework at that age. They begin with very simple tasks, but their responsibilities gradually increase. And they do these tasks without payment. Lancy contrasts this with what happens in America. \"We deny our children's bids to help until they are 6 or 7 years old,\" Lancy says, \"when many have lost the desire to help and then try to motivate them with payment. The solution to this problem is not to try to use money as an incentive to do housework, but to get children involved in housework much earlier, when they actually want to do it.\"", "53": "The practice of paying children an allowance became popular in America about 100 years ago. Nowadays, American kids on average receive about $800 per year in allowance. But the vast majority of American parents who pay allowance tie it to the completion of housework. Although many parents believe that paying an allowance for completing chores benefits their children, a range of experts expressed concern that tying allowance very closely to chores may not be ideal. In fact, the way chores work in many households worldwide points to another way.\nSuniya Luthar, a psychologist, is against paying kids for chores. Luthar is not opposed to giving allowances, but she thinks it's important to establish that chores are done not because they will lead to payment, but because they keep the household running. Luthar's suggested approach to allowance is compatible with that of writer Ron Lieber, who advises that allowances be used as a means of showing children how to save, give, and spend on things they care about. Kids should do chores, he writes, \"for the same reason adults do, because the chores need to be done, and not with the expectation of compensation.\"\nThis argument has its critics, but considering the way chores are undertaken around the world may change people's thinking. Professor David Lancy of Utah State University has studied how families around the world handle chores. At about 18 months of age, Lancy says, most children become eager to help their parents, and in many cultures, they begin helping with housework at that age. They begin with very simple tasks, but their responsibilities gradually increase. And they do these tasks without payment. Lancy contrasts this with what happens in America. \"We deny our children's bids to help until they are 6 or 7 years old,\" Lancy says, \"when many have lost the desire to help and then try to motivate them with payment. The solution to this problem is not to try to use money as an incentive to do housework, but to get children involved in housework much earlier, when they actually want to do it.\"", "54": "The practice of paying children an allowance became popular in America about 100 years ago. Nowadays, American kids on average receive about $800 per year in allowance. But the vast majority of American parents who pay allowance tie it to the completion of housework. Although many parents believe that paying an allowance for completing chores benefits their children, a range of experts expressed concern that tying allowance very closely to chores may not be ideal. In fact, the way chores work in many households worldwide points to another way.\nSuniya Luthar, a psychologist, is against paying kids for chores. Luthar is not opposed to giving allowances, but she thinks it's important to establish that chores are done not because they will lead to payment, but because they keep the household running. Luthar's suggested approach to allowance is compatible with that of writer Ron Lieber, who advises that allowances be used as a means of showing children how to save, give, and spend on things they care about. Kids should do chores, he writes, \"for the same reason adults do, because the chores need to be done, and not with the expectation of compensation.\"\nThis argument has its critics, but considering the way chores are undertaken around the world may change people's thinking. Professor David Lancy of Utah State University has studied how families around the world handle chores. At about 18 months of age, Lancy says, most children become eager to help their parents, and in many cultures, they begin helping with housework at that age. They begin with very simple tasks, but their responsibilities gradually increase. And they do these tasks without payment. Lancy contrasts this with what happens in America. \"We deny our children's bids to help until they are 6 or 7 years old,\" Lancy says, \"when many have lost the desire to help and then try to motivate them with payment. The solution to this problem is not to try to use money as an incentive to do housework, but to get children involved in housework much earlier, when they actually want to do it.\"", "55": "The practice of paying children an allowance became popular in America about 100 years ago. Nowadays, American kids on average receive about $800 per year in allowance. But the vast majority of American parents who pay allowance tie it to the completion of housework. Although many parents believe that paying an allowance for completing chores benefits their children, a range of experts expressed concern that tying allowance very closely to chores may not be ideal. In fact, the way chores work in many households worldwide points to another way.\nSuniya Luthar, a psychologist, is against paying kids for chores. Luthar is not opposed to giving allowances, but she thinks it's important to establish that chores are done not because they will lead to payment, but because they keep the household running. Luthar's suggested approach to allowance is compatible with that of writer Ron Lieber, who advises that allowances be used as a means of showing children how to save, give, and spend on things they care about. Kids should do chores, he writes, \"for the same reason adults do, because the chores need to be done, and not with the expectation of compensation.\"\nThis argument has its critics, but considering the way chores are undertaken around the world may change people's thinking. Professor David Lancy of Utah State University has studied how families around the world handle chores. At about 18 months of age, Lancy says, most children become eager to help their parents, and in many cultures, they begin helping with housework at that age. They begin with very simple tasks, but their responsibilities gradually increase. And they do these tasks without payment. Lancy contrasts this with what happens in America. \"We deny our children's bids to help until they are 6 or 7 years old,\" Lancy says, \"when many have lost the desire to help and then try to motivate them with payment. The solution to this problem is not to try to use money as an incentive to do housework, but to get children involved in housework much earlier, when they actually want to do it.\""}, "questions": [{"id": "2020-09-2-c-26", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 26, "stem": "", "options": ["A) associated", "B) coincidence", "C) determined", "D) drastically", "E) enormous", "F) imitate", "G) indication", "H) integrate", "I) maximizes", "J) natural", "K) principal", "L) recognizable", "M) simply", "N) stressful", "O) symbolizes"], "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H) integrate\n动词\n【词性槽】主语后缺谓语动词\n【依据】integrate ... in 表\"把……融入/采用\"，与\"在机舱座椅中采用各种蓝色\"贴合\nintegrate various shades of blue in their cabin seats 意为\"在机舱座椅中融入/采用各种\n蓝色调\"，与下句 and it is no __(27)__ 接的\"这并非巧合\"逻辑成立。\n【竞争词】F) imitate \"模仿各种蓝色\"语义荒谬\nimitate 意为\"模仿\"，对象通常是行为/风格/某人，把 various shades of blue 当模仿对象既不\n合逻辑，也与全文\"航司主动选用蓝色\"的主旨相悖。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2020-09-2-c-27", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 27, "stem": "", "options": ["A) associated", "B) coincidence", "C) determined", "D) drastically", "E) enormous", "F) imitate", "G) indication", "H) integrate", "I) maximizes", "J) natural", "K) principal", "L) recognizable", "M) simply", "N) stressful", "O) symbolizes"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B) coincidence\n名词\n【词性槽】no 后接名词的固定结构\n【依据】no coincidence 是固定搭配，呼应后句的心理学理由\n下一句 There does appear to be some psychology behind it 明确说航空公司选蓝色\n背后有心理学原因，所以前一句要表达\"这并非巧合\"，正是 it is no coincidence 的固定\n表达。\n【竞争词】G) indication 搭配与逻辑均不通\nindication 意为\"迹象、暗示\"，常用 an indication of/that，不与 no 构成定式表达。若填 no\nindication，意为\"没有任何迹象\"，与紧接其后的 There does appear to be some psychology\nbehind it 逻辑相反。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2020-09-2-c-28", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 28, "stem": "", "options": ["A) associated", "B) coincidence", "C) determined", "D) drastically", "E) enormous", "F) imitate", "G) indication", "H) integrate", "I) maximizes", "J) natural", "K) principal", "L) recognizable", "M) simply", "N) stressful", "O) symbolizes"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A) associated\n形容词\n【词性槽】be + ___ + with 的过去分词形容词\n【依据】be associated with 是英语标准搭配，表\"与……相关\"\nBlue is associated with the positive qualities ... = \"蓝色与信任、效率、安静等积极品质\n相关\"，直接对应全文心理学解释——颜色与情绪/特质的关联。\n【竞争词】C) determined be determined with 不成立的搭配，语义也别扭\ndetermined 作过去分词多用 determined by（由……决定），作形容词义为\"坚定的\"，与 with\n后接的 trust、efficiency 等品质组不出意思通顺的句子。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2020-09-2-c-29", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 29, "stem": "", "options": ["A) associated", "B) coincidence", "C) determined", "D) drastically", "E) enormous", "F) imitate", "G) indication", "H) integrate", "I) maximizes", "J) natural", "K) principal", "L) recognizable", "M) simply", "N) stressful", "O) symbolizes"], "answer": "L", "analysis": "【答案】L) recognizable\n形容词\n【词性槽】make + 宾语 + ___ 宾补位填形容词\n【依据】强化品牌就是让品牌更易识别\n上文 Our job as designers is to reinforce the airline's brand 明确说\"我们的工作是强\n化航司品牌\"，强化品牌的直接结果就是\"让它更可识别（more recognizable）\"，逻辑链\n紧凑。\n【竞争词】E) enormous \"让品牌更巨大\"在英语里不自然\nenormous 形容体量/规模，搭品牌不形成英语里的惯用表达，也与\"强化品牌识别度\"的真实业\n务目标不符。\nJ) natural 和品牌强化关系弱，且占位与 33 题冲突\nnatural 与 reinforce the brand 主题不贴；且 domestic, __(33)__ and earthy 处与\ndomestic、earthy 并列描述柔和色调，natural 在 33 题契合度高得多，应让给 33。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2020-09-2-c-30", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 30, "stem": "", "options": ["A) associated", "B) coincidence", "C) determined", "D) drastically", "E) enormous", "F) imitate", "G) indication", "H) integrate", "I) maximizes", "J) natural", "K) principal", "L) recognizable", "M) simply", "N) stressful", "O) symbolizes"], "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I) maximizes\n动词\n【词性槽】定语从句里第三人称单数主语后的谓语\n【依据】maximizes comfort 是常见搭配，与\"营造愉悦环境\"一致\nmaximizes comfort = \"最大化舒适度\"，与后续目的状语 to create a pleasant\nenvironment 形成因果链：把舒适做到最大，从而营造愉悦的环境。\n【竞争词】O) symbolizes \"内饰象征舒适\"语义牵强\nsymbolize 意为\"象征\"，其宾语通常是抽象概念被某具体事物代表，本句要表达的是机舱内饰实\n实在在地\"提供/最大化\"舒适，而非\"象征\"舒适；并且 symbolizes 与下文\"营造愉悦环境\"的因果\n逻辑薄弱。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2020-09-2-c-31", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 31, "stem": "", "options": ["A) associated", "B) coincidence", "C) determined", "D) drastically", "E) enormous", "F) imitate", "G) indication", "H) integrate", "I) maximizes", "J) natural", "K) principal", "L) recognizable", "M) simply", "N) stressful", "O) symbolizes"], "answer": "N", "analysis": "【答案】N) stressful\n形容词\n【词性槽】make + 名词 + ___ 中比较级 less 修饰形容词\n【依据】less stressful 与下句\"蓝色让人平静\"完美呼应\n紧随其后是 blue is said to induce a feeling of calm，蓝色诱发平静感，正是为了减少紧\n张感；less stressful = 更少压力，与 calm 形成同义呼应，逻辑严丝合缝。\n【竞争词】E) enormous 词义偏\"巨大\"，与本句焦点无关\nenormous 强调体量大，谈飞行体验\"less enormous\"既不通顺，也无法与下句 calm 形成\n对照。\nJ) natural 占位与第 33 空冲突\nnatural 在第 33 空与 domestic、earthy 并列形容\"家一般的柔和色调\"，搭配契合度远高于\n本空；本空若用 natural，less natural（更少自然感）反而是负向，与航空公司想营造的氛围\n相反。\nL) recognizable 词义指向品牌识别，与本句\"乘机体验\"无关\nrecognizable 意为\"易识别的\"，更适合配合品牌（见 29 题语境），与 less ___ 描写乘机感受\n不搭。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 J) —— 把 natural 误填到 31，又导致 33 没词可用。考生若按题号\n顺序做题，做到 making the traveling experience less __(31)__ 时只看本句容易选\nnatural（less natural 看似也通），但下文 domestic, __(33)__ and earthy 需要 natural\n与 domestic、earthy 并列，且 31 应承接下句 calm 的反向呼应，正确答案是 stressful。\n防坑：先做 33 题的并列锁定 natural，再回头解 31。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2020-09-2-c-32", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 32, "stem": "", "options": ["A) associated", "B) coincidence", "C) determined", "D) drastically", "E) enormous", "F) imitate", "G) indication", "H) integrate", "I) maximizes", "J) natural", "K) principal", "L) recognizable", "M) simply", "N) stressful", "O) symbolizes"], "answer": "K", "analysis": "【答案】K) principal\n形容词\n【词性槽】定冠词 The 后 + ___ + aim 中定语位\n【依据】principal aim = 主要目的，是常见英语搭配\nprincipal 作形容词意为\"主要的\"，principal aim/principal reason 是英语中固定的高频搭\n配，与下文 to create a home-like relaxing feel 直接挂钩——\"主要目的是营造家一般的\n放松感\"。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2020-09-2-c-33", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 33, "stem": "", "options": ["A) associated", "B) coincidence", "C) determined", "D) drastically", "E) enormous", "F) imitate", "G) indication", "H) integrate", "I) maximizes", "J) natural", "K) principal", "L) recognizable", "M) simply", "N) stressful", "O) symbolizes"], "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J) natural\n形容词\n【词性槽】三个并列形容词中间位\n【依据】与 domestic、earthy 同属\"家用自然柔和\"语义场\ndomestic（居家的）、earthy（朴实的、大地感的）都带\"自然、亲近、柔和\"色彩，\nnatural（自然的）与之同向并列，构成排比，正好呼应前句 create a home-like\nrelaxing feel。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2020-09-2-c-34", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 34, "stem": "", "options": ["A) associated", "B) coincidence", "C) determined", "D) drastically", "E) enormous", "F) imitate", "G) indication", "H) integrate", "I) maximizes", "J) natural", "K) principal", "L) recognizable", "M) simply", "N) stressful", "O) symbolizes"], "answer": "M", "analysis": "【答案】M) simply\n副词\n【词性槽】助动词 has 与过去分词 stuck 之间填副词\n【依据】\"就这么沿用下来\"贴合\"几十年前形成并延续至今\"的语义\n本句 It's also a trend that emerged decades ago and has __(34)__ stuck 强调这种用蓝\n色的趋势出现得早、之后就\"自然而然\"地保留下来；simply stuck 表达\"就这么固定下来\n了\"的语气，自然平实。\n【竞争词】D) drastically 程度过强，与\"沿袭\"的语气冲突\ndrastically 意为\"剧烈地、彻底地\"，多用于描述剧烈变化（drastically changed/dropped），\n而 stuck 表\"固定不变\"，二者在程度与方向上互相矛盾。", "points": ["选词填空", "副词"]}, {"id": "2020-09-2-c-35", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 35, "stem": "", "options": ["A) associated", "B) coincidence", "C) determined", "D) drastically", "E) enormous", "F) imitate", "G) indication", "H) integrate", "I) maximizes", "J) natural", "K) principal", "L) recognizable", "M) simply", "N) stressful", "O) symbolizes"], "answer": "O", "analysis": "【答案】O) symbolizes\n动词\n【词性槽】定语从句中单数先行词 shade 后的三单谓语\n【依据】颜色象征某种品质是英语里的典型表达\nsymbolize being trustworthy and safe = \"象征值得信任与安全\"，与全段\"蓝色被选作品\n牌色\"主旨吻合：颜色之所以被选，正因为它能在视觉上象征积极特质。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 I) —— 三单动词混淆，把 maximizes 误填到 35。maximizes 与\nsymbolizes 同为三单及物动词，若先做 35 题再做 30 题，考生可能在 a conservative ...\nshade that __(35)__ being trustworthy and safe 处填 maximizes，导致 30 题反过来选\nsymbolizes，整个段落语义崩盘。防坑：30 题的 maximize comfort 是更稳定的固定搭\n配，应先用过即划，35 题自然只剩 symbolizes。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2020-09-2-m-36", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 36, "stem": "While cultural holidays are celebrated, the injustices experienced by Asian Americans are not exposed in elementary school classrooms.", "options": null, "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】庆节日却避不公\n【定位】Elementary school classrooms celebrate cultural holidays—Lunar New Year! Red envelopes!\nLion dancers!—but they're quick to gloss over(掩饰) the challenges and injustices that Asian\nAmericans have faced.\n小学课堂会庆祝各种文化节日——春节！红包！舞狮！——但他们很快就掩饰过了亚裔美国\n人所面对的种种挑战和不公。\n【改写】cultural holidays are celebrated ↔ Elementary school classrooms celebrate cultural holidays\n被动主动同形替换\ninjustices experienced by Asian Americans are not exposed ↔\nquick to gloss over(掩饰) the challenges and injustices that Asian Americans have faced\n「不揭示」=「掩饰」\nelementary school classrooms ↔ Elementary school classrooms\n限定场所原词复现", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2020-09-2-m-37", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 37, "stem": "Little information can be found about Chinese immigrants in the author's school library.", "options": null, "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】学校图书馆资料少\n【定位】That was all there was in my entire school library in San Francisco, home of the nation's\nfirst Chinatown.\n在我那个位于旧金山、全美第一个唐人街所在地的整所学校图书馆里，就只有那么一页。\n【改写】Little information can be found about Chinese immigrants ↔\na page about early Chinese immigrants\n数量上位：一页=很少\nin the author's school library ↔ my entire school library\n人称转换同指", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2020-09-2-m-38", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 38, "stem": "A middle school teacher is making a great effort to help students learn about the contributions made by Chinese immigrants to America.", "options": null, "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】中学教师助学华人贡献\n【定位】Wong, a middle school teacher during the school year, hopes he's exposing the students to\nhow Chinese Americans contributed to the U.S., something that he didn't get as a student\ngrowing up in the San Francisco Bay Area.\nWong在学年期间是一位初中老师，他希望通过这种方式让学生看到华人对美国的贡献，这\n正是他自己在旧金山湾区长大时没有得到的教育。\n【改写】A middle school teacher is making a great effort ↔\nWong, a middle school teacher during the school year, hopes he's exposing the students\n身份原词+努力概括\ncontributions made by Chinese immigrants to America ↔\nhow Chinese Americans contributed to the U.S.\n名词化+国名同指\n【辨邻】 A A 段虽然出现 Chinese immigrants 与淘金主题，但讲的是作者四年级时的研究兴\n趣，并没有任何「中学教师做出努力让学生了解华人贡献」的事，缺少题干要求的「教\n师努力」这一动作主体。\nI was excited because I had asked to research Chinese immigrants during that era", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2020-09-2-m-39", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 39, "stem": "No Asian Americans were included in the list of historical figures recommended for study in K-12 classrooms.", "options": null, "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】推荐名单中零亚裔\n【定位】Of the nearly 100 Americans recommended to be studied, 77% were white, 18% were African\nAmerican, 4% were Native American, and 1% were Latino. None were Asian American.\n在大约100名被推荐供学习的美国人中，77%是白人、18%是非裔、4%是原住民、1%是拉\n丁裔，没有一个是亚裔美国人。\n【改写】No Asian Americans were included ↔ None were Asian American\nNo=None的否定等值\nlist of historical figures recommended for study in K-12 classrooms ↔\nnearly 100 Americans recommended to be studied\n推荐研究名单概括上位", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2020-09-2-m-40", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 40, "stem": "There is an obvious lack of teachers with a multicultural perspective to meet the curriculum challenges in America.", "options": null, "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】缺多元背景教师\n【定位】Teachers with a multicultural background or training could perhaps overcome such\ncurriculum challenges, but they're few and far between.\n有多元文化背景或培训的教师本可以化解这些课程难题，可惜这种教师寥寥无几。\n【改写】obvious lack of teachers with a multicultural perspective ↔\nTeachers with a multicultural background or training could perhaps overcome such\ncurriculum challenges, but they're few and far between\n习语「寥寥无几」=「明显缺乏」\ncurriculum challenges in America ↔ such curriculum challenges\n课程难题原词复现\n【辨邻】 I I 段强调 it's not so much about the teacher's background, but about training，\n是讲训练比背景重要的观点，没有出现「这种教师寥寥无几/缺乏」这一关键的「缺」\n字命题，因此不是源头段。\nYou can have a great curriculum but if you don't have teachers dedicated(专注于) to teaching\nit well", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2020-09-2-m-41", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 41, "stem": "Students of ethnic backgrounds learn better from a multicultural curriculum.", "options": null, "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】少数族裔学生学得更好\n【定位】Students of color are more engaged and earn better grades when they see themselves in\ntheir studies.\n有色人种学生在学习内容里看到自己的影子时，会更投入、也能取得更好的成绩。\n【改写】Students of ethnic backgrounds ↔ Students of color\n族裔学生的同义改写\nlearn better ↔ more engaged and earn better grades\n学习表现的概括上位\nfrom a multicultural curriculum ↔ gain from a multicultural curriculum\n多元课程原词复现", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2020-09-2-m-42", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 42, "stem": "Now more and more high schools in America are including ethnic studies in their curriculums.", "options": null, "answer": "K", "analysis": "【答案】K 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】越来越多高中开族裔课\n【定位】Some school districts, including San Francisco and Los Angeles, already offer ethnic studies\nat their high schools. High schools in Portland, Chicago, and elsewhere have either\nimplemented or will soon introduce ethnic studies classes.\n包括旧金山和洛杉矶在内的一些学区已经在高中开设了族裔研究课程。波特兰、芝加哥等地\n的高中也已经开设或即将开设这种课程。\n【改写】more and more high schools in America are including ethnic studies in their curriculums ↔\nHigh schools in Portland, Chicago, and elsewhere have either implemented or will soon\nintroduce ethnic studies classes\n列举上位为趋势\nincluding ethnic studies in their curriculums ↔ offer ethnic studies at their high schools\ninclude=offer的同义改写", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2020-09-2-m-43", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 43, "stem": "A study of some K-12 textbooks and teacher manuals showed that Asian Americans were inadequately and improperly represented in them.", "options": null, "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】K-12课本研究的发现\n【定位】His 2016 study of K-12 social studies textbooks and teacher manuals found that Asian\nAmericans were poorly represented at best, and subjected to racist caricatures(拙劣的模仿)\nat worst.\n他2016年对K-12社会研究教科书和教师手册的研究发现，亚裔美国人在书中往好里说也是\n呈现得不充分，往坏里说则被刻画成了带种族偏见的拙劣形象。\n【改写】study of some K-12 textbooks and teacher manuals ↔\nHis 2016 study of K-12 social studies textbooks and teacher manuals\n研究对象原词复现\nAsian Americans were inadequately and improperly represented ↔\nAsian Americans were poorly represented at best, and subjected to racist caricatures(拙劣的\n模仿) at worst\n两极程度对位为两个副词", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2020-09-2-m-44", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 44, "stem": "When taking a class in college, the author realized that a lot of information about Asian Americans was left out of the textbooks he studied.", "options": null, "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】大学课上的醒悟\n【定位】I finally had the opportunity to learn about Asian Americans like myself, and how we became\npart of the fabric of the United States when I took an introductory class on Asian-American\nhistory in college. The class was a revelation. I realized how much had been missing in my\ntextbooks as I grew up.\n直到我在大学修了一门亚裔美国人历史导论课，才终于有机会学到关于像我一样的亚裔美国\n人、以及我们如何成为美国社会一部分的内容。那门课让我恍然大悟。我意识到自己成长过\n程中的教科书里漏掉了多少东西。\n【改写】When taking a class in college ↔\nwhen I took an introductory class on Asian-American history in college\n时间状语原词复现\na lot of information about Asian Americans was left out of the textbooks ↔\nhow much had been missing in my textbooks\nleft out=missing的同义改写", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2020-09-2-m-45", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 45, "stem": "An Asian-American studies professor placed greater emphasis on teacher training than on teachers' background.", "options": null, "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】培训比背景重要\n【定位】She added that it's not so much about the teacher's background, but about training.\n她接着说，关键并不那么在于教师的背景，而在于培训。\n【改写】An Asian-American studies professor ↔\nAllyson Tintiangco-Cubales, an Asian-American studies professor at San Francisco State\nUniversity\n身份头衔原词复现\nplaced greater emphasis on teacher training than on teachers' background ↔\nit's not so much about the teacher's background, but about training\n「不那么…而是…」=「更强调」\n【辨邻】 H H 段虽然谈论 Teachers with a multicultural background or training，但论点是这\n种教师 few and far between（人少），强调的是「数量短缺」，没有提出「训练比\n背景更重要」这一价值排序判断，因此不是源头段。\nIt isn't a requirement that teachers share the same racial or ethnic background as their students, but\nthe imbalance poses challenges", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2020-09-2-r-46", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 46, "stem": "What do we learn from the passage about reverse graffiti?", "options": ["A) It uses paint to create anti-pollution images.", "B) It creates a lot of trouble for local residents.", "C) It causes lots of distraction to drivers.", "D) It turns dirty walls into artistic works."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What do we learn from the passage about reverse graffiti? 问的是关于这个概念\n的客观信息，答案应该是对原文某句的同义转述，不需要推断\n【拆句】题干 What do we learn from the passage about reverse graffiti? 是 What 引导的特殊\n疑问句，主干 we learn，from the passage 是状语，about reverse graffiti 修饰 learn 的\n内容；无从句、无否定、无比较，结构平铺直叙，无陷阱\n【定位】They find dirty surfaces and paint them with images or messages using cleaning brushes\nor pressure hoses. Either way, it's the same principle: the image is made by cleaning away\nthe dirt.\n他们找到脏污的表面，用清洁刷或高压水管在上面「画」出图像或讯息。无论哪种方式，原\n理都一样：图像是通过清除脏污而形成的。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\nuses paint to create anti-pollution images 把原文的核心做法说反了——反向涂鸦恰\n恰不用颜料，而是清除污渍\nB) 无中生有\ncreates a lot of trouble for local residents 文中无对应描述；麻烦的是市议会的执法\n逻辑，居民层面文中只提到他们 want to live in clean and attractive\nneighbourhoods，并未说反向涂鸦给居民带来麻烦\nC) 张冠李戴\n原文提到的是 Made up of a series of white skulls(颅骨), the painting reminds\ndrivers of the effect their pollution is having on the planet——是提醒司机，而非\n分散司机注意力；且这是 Orion 一人的做法，并非反向涂鸦的普遍特征\nD) ✓ 正确\nthe image is made by cleaning away the dirt ↔ turns dirty walls into artistic works\n预判要的是「在脏面上靠清洁形成图像」，turns dirty walls into artistic works 正是这\n层意思的同义改写", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-09-2-r-47", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 47, "stem": "What do reverse graffiti artists try to do?", "options": ["A) Publicise their artistic pursuit.", "B) Beautify the city environment.", "C) Raise public awareness of environmental pollution.", "D) Express their dissatisfaction with local governments."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What do reverse graffiti artists try to do? 问群体共同意图，原文必有明文交代，仅\n需找出该明文即可\n【拆句】题干 What do reverse graffiti artists try to do? 是 What 引导的特殊疑问句，主语\nreverse graffiti artists，谓语 try to do；try to do 是固定结构，表示「试图、致力于做某\n事」；句法平直，无陷阱\n【定位】Each artist has their own individual style but all artists share a common aim: to draw attention\nto the pollution in our cities.\n每个艺术家都有自己独特的风格，但所有艺术家都拥有一个共同的目标：唤起人们对城市污\n染的关注。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\nPublicise their artistic pursuit 把原文中的「让公众关注城市污染」偷换成「宣传他们\n自己的艺术追求」——原文 a common aim 是污染议题，不是他们个人艺术名声\nB) 无中生有\nBeautify the city environment 文中无对应；尽管反向涂鸦的产物确实清洁了表面，但\n艺术家自陈的目的是「让人关注污染」，而非「美化城市」\nC) ✓ 正确\nto draw attention to the pollution in our cities ↔\nRaise public awareness of environmental pollution\n预判要的是「让公众关注污染」，Raise public awareness of environmental\npollution 是其标准同义改写\nD) 过度推断\nExpress their dissatisfaction with local governments 把作者在第三段对市议会的暗\n讽错当成全体反向涂鸦艺术家的目的；原文意图句仅指向污染议题，并未把矛头对准\n政府", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-09-2-r-48", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 48, "stem": "What do we learn about Brazilian artist Alexandre Orion?", "options": ["A) He was good at painting white skulls.", "B) He chose tunnels to do his graffiti art.", "C) He suggested banning all polluting cars.", "D) He was fond of doing creative artworks."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What do we learn about Brazilian artist Alexandre Orion? 问的是该人物的客观情\n况，属于以人为锚的细节题\n【拆句】题干 What do we learn about Brazilian artist Alexandre Orion? 主干 we learn，about\n后跟同位语结构「Brazilian artist Alexandre Orion」，前者是身份、后者是姓名；结构清\n晰，无陷阱\n【定位】Brazilian artist, Alexandre Orion, turned one of Sao Paulo's transport tunnels into an amazing\nwall painting in 2006 by getting rid of the dirt.\n巴西艺术家亚历山大·奥利安在 2006 年通过清除污渍，把圣保罗的一条交通隧道变成了一幅\n令人惊叹的壁画。\n【选项】A) 过度推断\nwas good at painting white skulls 把原文中作品的视觉成分 a series of white skulls\n误说成「擅长画白颅骨」；原文从未评价他的技能水平，颅骨也只是这一幅作品的具体\n内容，并非他的招牌技能\nB) ✓ 正确\nturned one of Sao Paulo's transport tunnels into an amazing wall painting ↔\nchose tunnels to do his graffiti art\n预判要的是「他选了隧道做反向涂鸦」，chose tunnels to do his graffiti art 正是这层\n客观事实的同义改写\nC) 无中生有\nsuggested banning all polluting cars 文中无对应；Orion 的原话仅是批评驾驶者不顾\n环境代价 they don't give any consideration to the price their comfort has for the\nenvironment，并未提出禁止任何污染车辆的建议\nD) 以偏概全\nwas fond of doing creative artworks 把原文一次具体的隧道清洁创作扩大成「热衷于\n做创意艺术品」这一普遍性格描述；原文 P2 只提到一次具体行动与一次发言，未对其\n性格倾向作概括评价", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-09-2-r-49", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 49, "stem": "What does the author imply about Leeds City Council's decision?", "options": ["A) It is simply absurd.", "B) It is well-informed.", "C) It is rather unexpected.", "D) It is quite sensible."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （推断题）\n【判型】题干 What does the author imply about Leeds City Council's decision? 中 imply 是推\n断题的标志词，问作者对市议会决定的隐含态度\n【拆句】题干 What does the author imply about Leeds City Council's decision? 是 What 引导\n的特殊疑问句，主语 the author，谓语 imply，about 引导对象状语；imply 一词把本题定\n性为推断题，是关键信号\n【定位】Moose was ordered to \"clean up his act.\" How was he supposed to do this: by making all\nproperty he had cleaned dirty again?\n穆斯被勒令「整改其行为」。他该怎么做呢：把他已经清洁过的所有地方再弄脏一遍？\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nHow was he supposed to do this: by making all property he had cleaned dirty again? ↔\nsimply absurd\n预判要的是「作者觉得这决定荒诞」，simply absurd 是该态度的最直接形容词替换\nB) 与原文相反\nwell-informed（消息灵通、了解情况）是正面评价，与作者用归谬反问表达的否定态度\n方向相反\nC) 偷换概念\nrather unexpected 把作者的「不合理、说不通」误写成「出乎意料」；原文并未表达\n「我没想到他们会这么决定」，而是表达「这决定本身就站不住脚」\nD) 与原文相反\nquite sensible（相当明智）是正面评价，作者用归谬反问明显否定这一决定，方向相反", "points": ["仔细阅读", "推断题"]}, {"id": "2020-09-2-r-50", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 50, "stem": "How did Sao Paulo city officials handle Alexandre Orion's reverse graffiti?", "options": ["A) They made him clean all the tunnels in Sao Paulo.", "B) They took drastic action to ban all reverse graffiti.", "C) They charged him with polluting tunnels in the city.", "D) They made it impossible for him to practice his art."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】题干 How did Sao Paulo city officials handle Alexandre Orion's reverse graffiti? 问官\n方采取了什么客观行动，原文必有明文，按细节题处理\n【拆句】题干 How did Sao Paulo city officials handle Alexandre Orion's reverse graffiti? 是\nHow 引导的特殊疑问句，handle 意为「处理、应对」；主体是官方，宾语是艺术家的作\n品；结构清晰，无陷阱\n【定位】The city officials then decided to take drastic action. They not only cleaned the whole tunnel\nbut every tunnel in Sao Paulo.\n市政官员随后决定采取激烈手段。他们不仅清洁了那条隧道，还清洁了圣保罗的每一条\n隧道。\n【选项】A) 张冠李戴\nmade him clean all the tunnels 把清洁主体偷换成艺术家本人；原文 They not only\ncleaned the whole tunnel 明确主语是 they（官方），不是艺术家\nB) 过度推断\nban all reverse graffiti 把官方动作上升为「禁止反向涂鸦」的法规层面；原文 the\nauthorities were annoyed but could find nothing to charge him with 明确说找不\n到罪名，因此他们没有也无法颁布禁令，只是用清洁的方式釜底抽薪\nC) 与原文相反\ncharged him with polluting tunnels 与原文明文 the authorities were annoyed but\ncould find nothing to charge him with 直接矛盾——原文说「找不到罪名可控告」，\n选项却说「以污染隧道为罪起诉了他」\nD) ✓ 正确\nThey not only cleaned the whole tunnel but every tunnel in Sao Paulo ↔\nmade it impossible for him to practice his art\nThe city officials then decided to take drastic action ↔\nmade it impossible for him to practice his art\n预判要的是「让艺术家无法继续创作」，made it impossible for him to practice his\nart 正是该后果的客观表述；反向涂鸦的前提是脏面，官方清光所有脏面即从根本上让他\n无法操作", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-09-2-r-51", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 51, "stem": "What do some experts think about paying children for doing chores?", "options": ["A) It may benefit children in more ways than one.", "B) It may help children learn the worth of labor.", "C) It may not turn out to be the best thing to do.", "D) It may not be accepted by low-income parents."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What do some experts think about paying children for doing chores? 直接问\"某\n些专家怎么看\",指向原文里一处明确观点句,属于客观转述类细节。\n【拆句】题干 What do some experts think about paying children for doing chores? 是一个\nwh- 疑问句,主干是 What do some experts think,后接介词短语 about paying children\nfor doing chores 作 think 的对象,paying 是动名词、doing 是动名词作介词 for 的宾语;结\n构平铺直叙、无陷阱。\n【定位】Although many parents believe that paying an allowance for completing chores benefits their\nchildren, a range of experts expressed concern that tying allowance very closely to chores\nmay not be ideal.\n尽管许多父母认为按完成家务发零花钱对孩子有益,一批专家却表达了担忧,认为把零花钱与\n家务紧密挂钩可能并非理想做法。\n【选项】A) 张冠李戴\nIt may benefit children in more ways than one 说\"给孩子付钱做家务在不止一方面对\n孩子有益\",正是原文 many parents believe that paying an allowance for\ncompleting chores benefits their children 中父母的观点,而非专家的观点;父母与专家\n在原文里立场相反,这一项把\"父母信念\"安到了\"专家观点\"头上。\nB) 无中生有\nIt may help children learn the worth of labor 说付钱能让孩子学到\"劳动的价值\",原文\n从未在专家口中出现 worth of labor 或近义表达,属于凭常识脑补。\nC) ✓ 正确\nmay not be ideal ↔ may not turn out to be the best thing to do\ntying allowance very closely to chores ↔ paying children for doing chores\n选项 It may not turn out to be the best thing to do 与预判\"未必是最好的做法\"语义\n完全吻合,情态词 may 和否定 not 都对齐,故锁定为答案。\nD) 无中生有\nIt may not be accepted by low-income parents 说低收入父母可能不接受,原文通篇\n没有讨论 low-income parents 这一群体,纯属节外生枝。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-09-2-r-52", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 52, "stem": "According to Suniya Luthar, doing chores will help children learn to ____.", "options": ["A) Share family responsibilities", "B) Appreciate the value of work", "C) Cultivate the spirit of independence", "D) Manage domestic affairs themselves"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】题干 According to Suniya Luthar, doing chores will help children learn to ____. 用\nAccording to + 人名指明出处,要求补全她的观点细节。\n【拆句】题干 According to Suniya Luthar, doing chores will help children learn to ____. 主干是\ndoing chores will help children learn to ____,doing chores 是动名词作主语,help sb.\nlearn to do 是固定结构,空位接动词原形;结构平铺直叙、无陷阱。\n【定位】Luthar is not opposed to giving allowances, but she thinks it's important to establish\nthat chores are done not because they will lead to payment, but because they keep the\nhousehold running.\nLuthar 并不反对给零花钱,但她认为重要的是要明确:做家务不是因为家务会带来报酬,而是因\n为家务能让家庭正常运转。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nthey keep the household running ↔ share family responsibilities\n选项 share family responsibilities 与预判\"为家庭运转分担一份力\"的语义吻合,核心都\n是\"为家庭\"而非\"为钱\"或\"为自身\"。\nB) 无中生有\nappreciate the value of work 与 Q51 中的 learn the worth of labor 几乎同款,都是原\n文未出现的\"劳动价值\"口号,Luthar 强调的是\"为家庭运转\"而非\"劳动的价值\"。\nC) 偷换概念\ncultivate the spirit of independence(培养独立精神)是常见的家务教育口号,但 Luthar\n通篇没有谈 independence,她谈的是 household running(家庭运转),把焦点从\"家庭\"挪\n到了\"个人独立\"。\nD) 过度推断\nmanage domestic affairs themselves 说孩子自己\"管家务\",反身代词 themselves 强\n调\"独自\",原文只说让家庭运转,从未要求孩子独立\"打理家务\",且年龄段也对不上。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-09-2-r-53", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 53, "stem": "What does Ron Lieber think should be the goal of giving children allowances?", "options": ["A) To help to strengthen family ties.", "B) To teach them how to manage money.", "C) To motivate them to do more housework.", "D) To show parents' appreciation of their help."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What does Ron Lieber think should be the goal of giving children allowances?\n直接问 Lieber 认为发零花钱的目的是什么,原文必有对应观点。\n【拆句】题干 What does Ron Lieber think should be the goal of giving children allowances?\n是宾语从句嵌入疑问句的结构:What 实际是宾语从句 What should be the goal of giving\nchildren allowances 中的主语,Ron Lieber think 是插入语,giving children allowances 是\n动名词短语作 of 的宾语;结构稍复杂但无陷阱。\n【定位】Luthar's suggested approach to allowance is compatible with that of writer Ron Lieber, who\nadvises that allowances be used as a means of showing children how to save, give, and spend\non things they care about.\nLuthar 的零花钱主张与作家 Ron Lieber 的主张一致,Lieber 建议把零花钱用作教孩子如何\n储蓄、给予以及在他们在意的事情上花钱的手段。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\nTo help to strengthen family ties 说零花钱能加强家庭纽带,family ties(家庭纽带)在原\n文中无任何对应表述。\nB) ✓ 正确\nshowing children how to save, give, and spend on things they care about ↔\nTo teach them how to manage money\n选项 To teach them how to manage money 把 save/give/spend 三者上位化为\nmanage money,与预判\"教孩子理财\"完全吻合。\nC) 与原文相反\nTo motivate them to do more housework 说零花钱用来\"激励孩子多做家务\",正是\nLieber 与 Luthar 共同反对的观点(原文末引文 not with the expectation of\ncompensation),把他立场完全反转。\nD) 偷换概念\nTo show parents' appreciation of their help 说零花钱是为表达父母对孩子帮助的感\n谢,Lieber 谈的是\"教孩子\"而非\"父母表达感激\",方向完全错位。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-09-2-r-54", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 54, "stem": "What does David Lancy say about 18-month-olds?", "options": ["A) They have a natural instinct to help around the house.", "B) They are too young to request money for what they do.", "C) They should learn to understand family responsibilities.", "D) They need a little incentive to get involved in housework."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What does David Lancy say about 18-month-olds? 直接问 Lancy 关于 18 个月孩\n子说了什么,原文必有出处。\n【拆句】题干 What does David Lancy say about 18-month-olds? 是 wh- 疑问句,主干是 What\ndoes David Lancy say,about 18-month-olds 是介词短语作 say 的对象状语,18-month-\nolds 是带连字符的复合名词,意为\"18 个月大的孩子\";结构平铺直叙。\n【定位】At about 18 months of age, Lancy says, most children become eager to help their parents, and\nin many cultures, they begin helping with housework at that age.\nLancy 说,大约 18 个月大时,大多数孩子就变得渴望帮父母,在许多文化中,他们就是从这个年\n龄开始参与家务的。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nbecome eager to help their parents ↔ have a natural instinct to help around the house\n选项 They have a natural instinct to help around the house 与预判\"自发想帮父母做\n家务\"完全吻合,natural instinct 把 eager 的强度感与自发性都吃下来了。\nB) 无中生有\nThey are too young to request money for what they do 说他们太小不会要钱,too\nyoung to 是\"太……以至于不能\"结构,原文从未谈 18 个月孩子是否\"要钱\",纯属脑补。\nC) 偷换概念\nThey should learn to understand family responsibilities 用 should 提出应然主张,但\n原文 Lancy 是在描述 18 个月孩子\"已经渴望帮忙\"这一事实,而非主张他们\"应该去理解家\n庭责任\";动作主体也被悄悄换成了\"教孩子去理解\"。\nD) 与原文相反\nThey need a little incentive to get involved in housework 说他们需要\"一点激励\"才\n参与家务,这恰是 Lancy 反对的观点;原文明确说他们 become eager to help,根本不需\n要外在 incentive,而文末 Lancy 又直接说 not to try to use money as an incentive。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-09-2-r-55", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 55, "stem": "What does David Lancy advise American parents to do?", "options": ["A) Set a good example for children in doing housework.", "B) Make children do housework without compensation.", "C) Teach children how to do housework.", "D) Accept children's early bids to help."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What does David Lancy advise American parents to do? 问 Lancy 给美国父母的\n建议,原文末尾必有明示建议句。\n【拆句】题干 What does David Lancy advise American parents to do? 是 wh- 疑问句,主干是\nWhat does David Lancy advise,advise sb. to do sth. 是固定结构,American parents 是\nadvise 的宾语,to do 是不定式作宾补;What 实指 to do 后省去的动词宾语;结构平铺直叙。\n【定位】The solution to this problem is not to try to use money as an incentive to do housework, but to\nget children involved in housework much earlier, when they actually want to do it.\n对这个问题的解决办法,不是用钱当作做家务的激励,而是让孩子在他们真的想做家务的更早\n年龄就参与进来。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\nSet a good example for children in doing housework 说父母要做榜样,原文从未谈\nset a good example/role model 类内容,Lancy 谈的是\"时机\"而非\"示范\"。\nB) 以偏概全\nMake children do housework without compensation 说要让孩子无偿做家\n务;without compensation 虽呼应了 Lieber 的 not with the expectation of\ncompensation,但 Lancy 的核心建议不是\"无偿\",而是\"趁早\"(timing),把重点偏移到了\"是\n否给钱\"上。\nC) 偷换概念\nTeach children how to do housework 说教孩子\"怎么做家务\"的技能,原文重点是\"何时\n让他们做\"(timing)而非\"如何教他们做\"(skill);两者方向不同。\nD) ✓ 正确\nWe deny our children's bids to help ↔ Accept children's early bids to help\nget children involved in housework much earlier, when they actually want to do it ↔\nAccept children's early bids to help\n选项 Accept children's early bids to help 把 deny → accept 反义对换,early、bids to\nhelp 原词保留,与预判\"趁早接住孩子主动\"完全吻合。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}], "listeningSharedWith": "2020-09-1"});
